Imagine reporting online abuse, explaining what happened and sharing your experience with the hope of getting help. But after taking that difficult step, you are still left wondering whether anything will change.

For survivors of technology-facilitated violence, reporting is only one part of the journey. What follows matters just as much: whether the complaint is taken seriously, whether support is provided and whether there is meaningful accountability.

The Digital Harm Effect report examines survivors’ satisfaction with the support they received. In Nigeria, 41.4% of respondents reported being satisfied with the support received.

This finding invites us to look beyond whether reporting channels exist. We must also ask whether the response survivors receive meets their needs and helps them feel supported.

If a woman reports abuse but is left without meaningful support, can we say justice has been served?

A survivor-centred response must go beyond receiving complaints. It should provide clear guidance, respectful treatment, appropriate support and meaningful steps towards accountability.

Reporting should be the beginning of a response, not the end of the conversation.

